== What unblocks it (STANDING-ORDERS §3; a recable, the Test Engineer's)
The case needs a BPDU-transparent path between two x230 ports. Exact change, either:
  (a) direct loop on the x230: take the AT-SPSX out of stack port3.0.7 (free, notconnect) into x230 port1.0.6, and the AT-SPSX
      out of stack port4.0.7 (free, notconnect) into x230 port1.0.7; patch ONE multimode LC-LC lead from x230 port1.0.6 to
      x230 port1.0.7. (Both cages are empty today; nothing else on the bench moves.)
  (b) or a hub / unmanaged switch with two x230 ports cabled into it.
Then the run is: RSTP on the x230 with port1.0.6/1.0.7 in one VLAN -> port1.0.7 Backup Discarding; eth3 broadcast into that
VLAN (port1.0.5 moved in) and the port1.0.7 TX counter flat.

Run: confounded -- the bench's only candidate "hub/switch" (the IE520 stack, STP off) does not pass BPDUs, so no loop could
  be built safely; nothing of steps 2-5 was measured.
VERDICT: PARTIAL -- the IE520 stack (STP disabled) terminates BPDUs, so x230 port1.0.4 never saw its own BPDU (stayed Designated Forwarding 20 s) and the loop could not be closed safely; needs a direct x230 port-to-port lead or a hub (recable, Test Engineer's)
